  </PageShell>\n```\n\nWait, I think I see the issue. Looking at the structure:\n\n1. We have a `<PageShell>` component\n2. Inside it, we have a `<div className=\"as-page\">`\n3. Inside that, we have a `<div className=\"as-section-row row g-4\">`\n4. Inside that, we have two main divs:\n   - First: `<div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">` (timeline section)\n   - Second: `<div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">` (team section)\n\nThe issue might be that we have elements at the same nesting level that aren't properly wrapped.\n\nLet me look at the exact structure around the error. The error is at line 240:14, which corresponds to the closing `</ResponsiveCard>` tag for the team section.\n\nActually, let me check if the issue is that we're missing a wrapper around the two columns. In a proper Bootstrap grid, we should have:\n\n```jsx\n<div className=\"as-page\">\n  <div className=\"as-section-row row g-4\">\n    <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\"> {/* Timeline */} </div>\n    <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\"> {/* Team */} </div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n```\n\nThis looks correct. But wait - let me check if there's an issue with the team section mapping function.\n\nLooking at the team section:\n```jsx\n{team.map((m, idx) => (\n  <div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>\n    ...\n    {m.bio && (\n      <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\n        <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: '.875rem', color: '#6b7280' }}>{m.bio}</p>\n      </div>\n    )}\n  </div>\n))}\n```\n\nAh! I see the issue now. The problem is with this part:\n```jsx\n{m.bio && (\n  <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\n    <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: '.875rem', color: '#6b7280' }}>{m.bio}</p>\n  </div>\n)}\n```\n\nWhen `m.bio` is falsy (empty string, null, undefined), this expression evaluates to `false`. And when you have `false` in JSX, it doesn't render anything - which is fine. But the issue is that we're returning `false` as a JSX element, and that's causing the \"adjacent JSX elements\" error.\n\nIn JSX, when you have an array of elements from a map, and you conditionally render something that can return `false`, React sees this as adjacent elements: the `<div>` element and the `false` value.\n\nThe fix is to explicitly return `null` when the condition is falsy, or use a ternary operator.\n\nLet me fix this:\n```jsx\n{m.bio ? (\n  <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\n    <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: '.875rem', color: '#6b7280' }}>{m.bio}</p>\n  </div>\n) : null}\n```\n\nActually, even better - we can just use the `&&` operator but make sure we're not returning a boolean. The issue is that `m.bio` might be an empty string, which is falsy but not null/undefined.\n\nLet me check what the actual values are. But regardless, the safest approach is to explicitly check for truthiness and return null otherwise.\n\nLet me fix both the timeline and team sections where this pattern occurs.\n\nActually, let me first just fix the team section since that's where the error is occurring according to the line number.\n\nLooking at line 240:14, that's the `</ResponsiveCard>` closing tag. So the error is in the team section mapping.\n\nLet me make the fix:\n", "signature": ""}, {"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-8a7ffceb10934075", "name": "Edit", "input": {"replace_all": false, "file_path": "C:\\Users\\01ker\\ArbiterCoffeeHUB\\frontend\\src\\pages\\admin\\AdminSettings.jsx", "old_string": "                        {m.bio && (\n                          <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\n                            <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: '.875rem', color: '#6b7280' }}>{m.bio}</p>\n                          </div>\n                        )}", "new_string": "                        {m.bio ? (\n                          <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\n                            <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: '.875rem', color: '#6b7280' }}>{m.bio}</p>\n                          </div>\n                        ) : null}"}}], "reasoning_content": null}, {"role": "