 priority\\n\\nIf CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md, or AGENTS.md says \"don\\'t use TDD\" and a skill says \"always use TDD,\" follow the user\\'s instructions. The user is in control.\\n\\n## How to Access Skills\\n\\n**In Claude Code:** Use the `Skill` tool. When you invoke a skill, its content is loaded and presented to you\u2014follow it directly. Never use the Read tool on skill files.\\n\\n**In Copilot CLI:** Use the `skill` tool. Skills are auto-discovered from installed plugins. The `skill` tool works the same as Claude Code\\'s `Skill` tool.\\n\\n**In Gemini CLI:** Skills activate via the `activate_skill` tool. Gemini loads skill metadata at session start and activates the full content on demand.\\n\\n**In other environments:** Check your platform\\'s documentation for how skills are loaded.\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nSkills use Claude Code tool names. Non-CC platforms: see `references/copilot-tools.md` (Copilot CLI), `references/codex-tools.md` (Codex) for tool equivalents. Gemini CLI users get the tool mapping loaded automatically via GEMINI.md.\\n\\n# Using Skills\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action.** Even a 1% chance a skill might apply means that you should invoke the skill to check. If an invoked skill turns out to be wrong for the situation, you don\\'t need to use it.\\n\\n```dot\\ndigraph skill_flow {\\n    \"User message received\" [shape=doublecircle];\\n    \"About to EnterPlanMode?\" [shape=doublecircle];\\n    \"Already brainstormed?\" [shape=diamond];\\n    \"Invoke brainstorming skill\" [shape=box];\\n    \"Might any skill apply?\" [shape=diamond];\\n    \"Invoke Skill tool\" [shape=box];\\n    \"Announce: \\'Using [skill] to [purpose]\\'\" [shape=box];\\n    \"Has checklist?\" [shape=diamond];\\n    \"Create TodoWrite todo per item\" [shape=box];\\n    \"Follow skill exactly\" [shape=box];\\n    \"Respond (including clarifications)\" [shape=doublecircle];\\n\\n    \"About to EnterPlanMode?\" -> \"Already brainstormed?\";\\n    \"Already brainstormed?\" -> \"Invoke brainstorming skill\" [label=\"no\"];\\n    \"Already brainstormed?\" -> \"Might any skill apply?\" [label=\"yes\"];\\n    \"Invoke brainstorming skill\" -> \"Might any skill apply?\";\\n\\n    \"User message received\" -> \"Might any skill apply?\";\\n    \"Might any skill apply?\" -> \"Invoke Skill tool\" [label=\"yes, even 1%\"];\\n    \"Might any skill apply?\" -> \"Respond (including clarifications)\" [label=\"definitely not\"];\\n    \"Invoke Skill tool\" -> \"Announce: \\'Using [skill] to [purpose]\\'\";\\n    \"Announce: \\'Using [skill] to [purpose]\\'\" -> \"Has checklist?\";\\n    \"Has checklist?\" -> \"Create TodoWrite todo per item\" [label=\"yes\"];\\n    \"Has checklist?\" -> \"Follow skill exactly\" [label=\"no\"];\\n    \"Create TodoWrite todo per item\" -> \"Follow skill exactly\";\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP\u2014you\\'re rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \"This doesn\\'t need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \"This doesn\\'t count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \"I\\'ll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept \u2260 using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills could apply, use this order:\\n\\n1. **Process skills first** (brainstorming, debugging) - these determine HOW to approach the task\\n2. **Implementation skills second** (frontend-design, mcp-builder) - these guide execution\\n\\n\"Let\\'s build X\" \u2192 brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n\"Fix this bug\" \u2192 debugging first, then domain-specific skills.\\n\\n## Skill Types\\n\\n**Rigid** (TDD, debugging): Follow exactly. Don\\'t adapt away discipline.\\n\\n**Flexible** (patterns): Adapt principles to context.\\n\\nThe skill itself tells you which.\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nInstructions say WHAT, not HOW. \"Add X\" or \"Fix Y\" doesn\\'t mean skip workflows.\\n\\n\\nARGUMENTS: continue'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '', 'reasoning_content': 'The user is asking me to continue with the mobile-first responsive UI/UX refactor task. I need to fix the remaining JSX syntax errors and import issues to enable a successful build.\\n\\nFrom the error output I saw earlier, the main remaining issue is in AdminSettings.jsx: \"Syntax error: Adjacent JSX elements must be wrapped in an enclosing tag. Did you want a JSX fragment <>...</>? (240:14)\"\\n\\nLet me look at the AdminSettings.jsx file more carefully to understand the structure and fix this issue.\\n\\nLooking at the file, I can see the structure:\\n- PageShell\\n  - div.as-page\\n    - div.as-section-row.row.g-4\\n      - div.col-12.col-sm-6 (Timeline section)\\n        - ResponsiveCard\\n          - div.as-section-head\\n          - div.as-section-body\\n      - div.col-12.col-sm-6 (Team members section) \\n        - ResponsiveCard\\n          - div.as-section-head\\n          - div.as-section-body\\n\\nThe error at line 240:14 suggests that there are adjacent JSX elements at that position. Let me examine what\\'s around line 240.\\n\\nFrom the file content I can see:\\n```\\n                </div>\\n              </ResponsiveCard>\\n            </div>\\n          </div>\\n        </div>\\n      </PageShell>\\n```\\n\\nThis looks like it should be properly nested. But let me check if there\\'s an issue with the closing tags.\\n\\nActually, looking more carefully at the structure, I think the issue might be that we have two root elements inside the PageShell. Let me check the return statement:\\n\\n```jsx\\nreturn (\\n  <PageShell title=\"Settings\" subtitle=\"Manage company timeline and team members\" error={error} onRetry={() => { fetchTimeline(); fetchTeam(); }}>\\n    <div className=\"as-page\">\\n      <div className=\"as-section-row row g-4\">\\n        {/* \u2500\u2500 Company Timeline \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 */}\\n        <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">\\n          <ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">\\n            ...\\n          </ResponsiveCard>\\n        </div>\\n\\n        {/* \u2500\u2500 Team Members \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 */}\\n        <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">\\n          <ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">\\n            ...\\n          </ResponsiveCard>\\n        </div>\\n      </div>\\n    </div>\\n  </PageShell>\\n);\\n```\\n\\nThis structure looks correct - there\\'s only one root element (the div with className=\"as-page\") inside the PageShell.\\n\\nWait, let me look at the actual error line again. The error says line 240:14. Let me see what\\'s at that exact position.\\n\\nLooking at the end of the file:\\n```\\n230                          </div>\\n231                        </div>\\n232                        {m.bio && (\\n233                          <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\\n234                            <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: \\'.875rem\\', color: \\'#6b7280\\' }}>{m.bio}</p>\\n235                          </div>\\n236                        )}\\n237                      </div>\\n238                    ))}\\n239                </div>\\n240              </ResponsiveCard>\\n241            </div>\\n242          </div>\\n243        </div>\\n244      </PageShell>\\n245  );\\n246};\\n```\\n\\nLine 240 is `</ResponsiveCard>`. The error says \"Adjacent JSX elements must be wrapped in an enclosing tag\" at this position.\\n\\nThis suggests that at line 240, there are two JSX elements sitting next to each other. But looking at the structure, line 239 is `</div>` (closing the as-section-body), line 240 is `</ResponsiveCard>` (closing the ResponsiveCard), line 241 is `</div>` (closing the col-12 col-sm-6), etc.\\n\\nActually, let me think about this differently. The error might be indicating that there\\'s a missing opening tag somewhere, causing the parser to see adjacent elements when it shouldn\\'t.\\n\\nLet me look at the team member section structure more carefully. Starting from line 188:\\n\\n```jsx\\n          {/* \u2500\u2500 Team Members \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 */}\\n          <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">\\n            <ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">\\n              <div className=\"as-section-head\">\\n                ...\\n              </div>\\n              <div className=\"as-section-body\">\\n                <ResponsiveAlert show={!!alert.team?.msg} onHide={() => clearAlert(\\'team\\')} message={alert.team?.msg} type={alert.team?.type} />\\n                {loading.team ? (\\n                  <div className=\"as-loading\"><div className=\"as-spinner\" />Loading team members\u2026</div>\\n                ) : team.length === 0 ? (\\n                  <div className=\"as-empty\">\\n                    <div className=\"as-empty-icon\"><FaUsers /></div>\\n                    <div className=\"as-empty-text\">No team members yet</div>\\n                    <div className=\"as-empty-sub\">Add members to display on your public page</div>\\n                  </div>\\n                ) : (\\n                  <div className=\"as-entries\">\\n                    {team.map((m, idx) => (\\n                      <div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>\\n                        <div className=\"as-entry-head\">\\n                          <div className=\"as-entry-num\">{idx + 1}</div>\\n                          {m.photo_url\\n                            ? <img className=\"as-avatar-preview\" src={m.photo_url} alt={m.name} loading=\"lazy\" style={{ width: 32, height: 32, borderRadius: \\'50%\\', objectFit: \\'cover\\' }} />\\n                            : <div className=\"as-avatar-placeholder\" style={{ width: 32, height: 32, fontSize: \\'.9rem\\' }}><FaUserCircle /></div>}\\n                          <span className={`as-entry-title ${m.name ? \\'\\' : \\'muted\\'}`}>\\n                            {m.name || \\'Unnamed\\'}\\n                            {m.role && <span style={{ fontWeight: 400, color: \\'#9ca3af\\', marginLeft: \\'.4rem\\', fontSize: \\'.8rem\\' }}>\u2014 {m.role}</span>}\\n                          </span>\\n                          <div className=\"as-entry-actions\">\\n                            <button className=\"as-icon-btn\" title=\"Edit\" onClick={() => openTmEdit(m)}><FaEdit /></button>\\n                            <button className=\"as-icon-btn danger\" title=\"Remove\" onClick={() => deleteTm(m)}><FaTrash /></button>\\n                          </div>\\n                        </div>\\n                        {m.bio && (\\n                          <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\\n                            <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: \\'.875rem\\', color: \\'#6b7280\\' }}>{m.bio}</p>\\n                          </div>\\n                        )}\\n                      </div>\\n                    ))}\\n                  </div>\\n                )}\\n              </div>\\n            </ResponsiveCard>\\n          </div>\\n```\\n\\nLooking at this, I see a potential issue. In the `{m.bio && (...)}` section, we have:\\n```jsx\\n{m.bio && (\\n  <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\\n    <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: \\'.875rem\\', color: \\'#6b7280\\' }}>{m.bio}</p>\\n  </div>\\n)}\\n```\\n\\nThis looks correct. But let me check if there\\'s a missing closing parenthesis or curly brace.\\n\\nActually, looking at line 238: `)}` - this is closing both the map function\\'s arrow function and the && condition. That looks right.\\n\\nWait, I think I see the issue. Let me look at the very end of the team member section:\\n\\nLines 230-244:\\n```\\n230                          </div>\\n231                        </div>\\n232                        {m.bio && (\\n233                          <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\\n234                            <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: \\'.875rem\\', color: \\'#6b7280\\' }}>{m.bio}</p>\\n235                          </div>\\n236                        )}\\n237                      </div>\\n238                    ))}\\n239                </div>\\n240              </ResponsiveCard>\\n241            </div>\\n242          </div>\\n243        </div>\\n244      </PageShell>\\n```\\n\\nLet me trace the opening and closing tags:\\n\\n1. Line 189: `<div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">` (opens column)\\n2. Line 190: `<ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">` (opens card)\\n3. Line 191: `<div className=\"as-section-head\">` (opens head)\\n4. Line 203: `</div>` (closes head)\\n5. Line 204: `<div className=\"as-section-body\">` (opens body)\\n6. Line 205-238: Alert, loading/empty/entries content\\n7. Line 239: `</div>` (closes body) \\n8. Line 240: `</ResponsiveCard>` (closes card)\\n9. Line 241: `</div>` (closes column)\\n10. Line 242: `</div>` (closes row)\\n11. Line 243: `</div>` (closes page)\\n12. Line 244: `</PageShell>` (closes PageShell)\\n\\nThis looks correct. But wait - let me double-check the timeline section to see if there\\'s a matching issue there.\\n\\nLooking at the timeline section (lines 135-186):\\n```\\n135            <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">\\n136              <ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">\\n137                <div className=\"as-section-head\">\\n138                ...\\n186            </ResponsiveCard>\\n187          </div>\\n```\\n\\nTimeline section:\\n- Line 135: opens column div\\n- Line 136: opens ResponsiveCard\\n- Line 137: opens as-section-head\\n- ... head content ...\\n- Line ~150: closes as-section-head \\n- Line ~151: opens as-section-body\\n- ... body content (timeline entries) ...\\n- Line ~185: closes as-section-body\\n- Line 186: closes ResponsiveCard\\n- Line 187: closes column div\\n\\nSo both sections look structurally correct.\\n\\nLet me try a different approach. Maybe the issue is that there\\'s actually content after the closing PageShell tag or before the return statement that\\'s causing the adjacent elements error.\\n\\nActually, let me look at the very beginning of the return statement and the very end.\\n\\nFrom line 130:\\n```\\n130\\t  return (\\n131\\t    <PageShell title=\"Settings\" subtitle=\"Manage company timeline and team members\" error={error} onRetry={() => { fetchTimeline(); fetchTeam(); }}>\\n132\\t      <div className=\"as-page\">\\n133\\t        <div className=\"as-section-row row g-4\">\\n134\\t          {/* \u2500\u2500 Company Timeline \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 */}\\n135\\t          <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">\\n136\\t            <ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">\\n...\\n244\\t    </PageShell>\\n245\\t  );\\n246\\t};\\n247\\t\\n248\\texport default AdminSettings;\\n```\\n\\nThis looks correct - the PageShell opens at line 131 and closes at line 244.\\n\\nWait, I think I might have it. Let me check if there are any stray characters or content outside the JSX.\\n\\nActually, let me try a simpler approach. Since the error is specifically about adjacent JSX elements, and it\\'s pointing to line 240, let me see if wrapping the content in a React fragment fixes it, and then we can work backwards.\\n\\nBut first, let me check if the issue is in the timeline section instead and the error is misleading.\\n\\nLet me look at the timeline section ending:\\n\\nAround lines 180-187:\\n```\\n180\\t                        )}\\n181\\t                      </div>\\n182\\t                    ))}\\n183\\t                  </div>\\n184\\t                )}\\n185              </ResponsiveCard>\\n186            </div>\\n187\\n188\\t          {/* \u2500\u2500 Team Members \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 */}\\n```\\n\\nThis looks correct too.\\n\\nLet me try to fix this by ensuring that we have proper nesting. Maybe the issue is that we\\'re missing a wrapper around the two columns.\\n\\nActually, in Bootstrap, when you use columns, they should be wrapped in a row, which they are (line 133: `<div className=\"as-section-row row g-4\">`). And that row should be wrapped in a container, which it is (line 132: `<div className=\"as-page\">`).\\n\\nBut let me check if the as-page div is actually needed or if it\\'s causing issues.\\n\\nLet me try removing the as-page div and see if that helps, or alternatively, let me try wrapping the two columns in an additional div to make sure there\\'s only one child of the row.\\n\\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the error: \"Adjacent JSX elements must be wrapped in an enclosing tag. Did you want a JSX fragment <>...</>?\"\\n\\nThis error occurs when you have something like:\\n```jsx\\nreturn (\\n  <div>Element 1</div>\\n  <div>Element 2</div>\\n);\\n```\\n\\nInstead of:\\n```jsx\\nreturn (\\n  <div>\\n    <div>Element 1</div>\\n    <div>Element 2</div>\\n  </div>\\n);\\n```\\n\\nOr using fragments:\\n```jsx\\nreturn (\\n  <>\\n    <div>Element 1</div>\\n    <div>Element 2</div>\\n  </>\\n);\\n```\\n\\nSo the error suggests that at the location of the error, there are two sibling JSX elements that aren\\'t wrapped in a common parent.\\n\\nGiven that the error is at line 240 (the `</ResponsiveCard>`), let me see what JSX elements are adjacent around there.\\n\\nActually, let me think about when this error typically occurs. It often happens when you have a conditional that returns JSX incorrectly, or when you\\'re mapping and accidentally return adjacent elements.\\n\\nLet me look again at the team member mapping section:\\n\\n```jsx\\n{team.map((m, idx) => (\\n  <div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>\\n    ...\\n  </div>\\n))}\\n```\\n\\nThis should be fine - the map function returns a single JSX element (the div) for each item.\\n\\nWait, unless... what if there\\'s an issue with the parentheses? Let me check the exact structure:\\n\\n```jsx\\n{team.map((m, idx) => (\\n  <div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>\\n    <div className=\"as-entry-head\">\\n      ...\\n    </div>\\n    {m.bio && (\\n      <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\\n        <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: \\'.875rem\\', color: \\'#6b7280\\' }}>{m.bio}</p>\\n      </div>\\n    )}\\n  </div>\\n))}\\n```\\n\\nLooking at this, I see a potential issue! The `{m.bio && (...)}` is inside the `as-entry-card` div, which is correct. But let me check if the parentheses are balanced.\\n\\nWe have:\\n- `team.map((m, idx) => (` - opens map arrow function\\n- `<div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>` - opens entry card div\\n- `<div className=\"as-entry-head\">` - opens entry head\\n- ... head content ...\\n- `</div>` - closes entry head\\n- `{m.bio && (` - opens bio condition\\n- `<div className=\"as-entry-body\">` - opens entry body\\n- `<p>...</p>` - paragraph\\n- `</div>` - closes entry body\\n- `)}` - closes bio condition AND map arrow function\\n- `</div>` - closes entry card div\\n- `))` - closes map function\\n\\nWait, that looks wrong! We have `)}` followed by `</div>` followed by `))`.\\n\\nLet me retrace:\\n1. `team.map((m, idx) => (` - opens map arrow function (expects closing `)`)\\n2. `<div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>` - opens div\\n3. `<div className=\"as-entry-head\">` - opens head div\\n4. ... head content ...\\n5. `</div>` - closes head div\\n6. `{m.bio && (` - opens bio condition (expects closing `)`)\\n7. `<div className=\"as-entry-body\">` - opens body div\\n8. `<p>...</p>` - paragraph\\n9. `</div>` - closes body div\\n10. `)` - closes bio condition\\n11. `</div>` - closes entry card div\\n12. `)` - closes map arrow function\\n13. `}` - closes the curly braces of the JSX expression\\n\\nSo we should have: `{team.map((m, idx) => ( ... ))}`\\n\\nWhich means after the entry card div closes, we should have `)` to close the map arrow function, then `}` to close the JSX braces.\\n\\nBut in the code at line 238, I see `)}` which would be closing the bio condition and then... what?\\n\\nLet me look at the actual lines again:\\n```\\n236                   