r, which it is (line 132: `<div className=\"as-page\">`).\n\nBut let me check if the as-page div is actually needed or if it's causing issues.\n\nLet me try removing the as-page div and see if that helps, or alternatively, let me try wrapping the two columns in an additional div to make sure there's only one child of the row.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the error: \"Adjacent JSX elements must be wrapped in an enclosing tag. Did you want a JSX fragment <>...</>?\"\n\nThis error occurs when you have something like:\n```jsx\nreturn (\n  <div>Element 1</div>\n  <div>Element 2</div>\n);\n```\n\nInstead of:\n```jsx\nreturn (\n  <div>\n    <div>Element 1</div>\n    <div>Element 2</div>\n  </div>\n);\n```\n\nOr using fragments:\n```jsx\nreturn (\n  <>\n    <div>Element 1</div>\n    <div>Element 2</div>\n  </>\n);\n```\n\nSo the error suggests that at the location of the error, there are two sibling JSX elements that aren't wrapped in a common parent.\n\nGiven that the error is at line 240 (the `</ResponsiveCard>`), let me see what JSX elements are adjacent around there.\n\nActually, let me think about when this error typically occurs. It often happens when you have a conditional that returns JSX incorrectly, or when you're mapping and accidentally return adjacent elements.\n\nLet me look again at the team member mapping section:\n\n```jsx\n{team.map((m, idx) => (\n  <div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>\n    ...\n  </div>\n))}\n```\n\nThis should be fine - the map function returns a single JSX element (the div) for each item.\n\nWait, unless... what if there's an issue with the parentheses? Let me check the exact structure:\n\n```jsx\n{team.map((m, idx) => (\n  <div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>\n    <div className=\"as-entry-head\">\n      ...\n    </div>\n    {m.bio && (\n      <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\n        <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: '.875rem', color: '#6b7280' }}>{m.bio}</p>\n      </div>\n    )}\n  </div>\n))}\n```