u2014 {m.role}</span>}\n                          </span>\n                          <div className=\"as-entry-actions\">\n                            <button className=\"as-icon-btn\" title=\"Edit\" onClick={() => openTmEdit(m)}><FaEdit /></button>\n                            <button className=\"as-icon-btn danger\" title=\"Remove\" onClick={() => deleteTm(m)}><FaTrash /></button>\n                          </div>\n                        </div>\n                        {m.bio && (\n                          <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\n                            <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: '.875rem', color: '#6b7280' }}>{m.bio}</p>\n                          </div>\n                        )}\n                      </div>\n                    ))}\n                  </div>\n                )}\n              </div>\n            </ResponsiveCard>\n          </div>\n```\n\nLooking at this, I see a potential issue. In the `{m.bio && (...)}` section, we have:\n```jsx\n{m.bio && (\n  <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\n    <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: '.875rem', color: '#6b7280' }}>{m.bio}</p>\n  </div>\n)}\n```\n\nThis looks correct. But let me check if there's a missing closing parenthesis or curly brace.\n\nActually, looking at line 238: `)}` - this is closing both the map function's arrow function and the && condition. That looks right.\n\nWait, I think I see the issue. Let me look at the very end of the team member section:\n\nLines 230-244:\n```\n230                          </div>\n231                        </div>\n232                        {m.bio && (\n233                          <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\n234                            <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: '.875rem', color: '#6b7280' }}>{m.bio}</p>\n235                          </div>\n236                        )}\n237                      </div>\n238                    ))}\n239                </div>\n240              </ResponsiveCard>\n241            </div>\n242          </div>\n243        </div>\n244      </PageShell>\n```\n\nLet me trace the opening and closing tags:\n\n1. Line 189: `<div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">` (opens column)\n2. Line 190: `<ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">` (opens card)\n3. Line 191: `<div className=\"as-section-head\">` (opens head)\n4. Line 203: `</div>` (closes head)\n5. Line 204: `<div className=\"as-section-body\">` (opens body)\n6. Line 205-238: Alert, loading/empty/entries content\n7. Line 239: `</div>` (closes body) \n8. Line 240: `</ResponsiveCard>` (closes card)\n9. Line 241: `</div>` (closes column)\n10. Line 242: `</div>` (closes row)\n11. Line 243: `</div>` (closes page)\n12. Line 244: `</PageShell>` (closes PageShell)\n\nThis looks correct. But wait - let me double-check the timeline section to see if there's a matching issue there.\n\nLooking at the timeline section (lines 135-186):\n```\n135            <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">\n136              <ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">\n137                <div className=\"as-section-head\">\n138                ...\n186            </ResponsiveCard>\n187          </div>\n```\n\nTimeline section:\n- Line 135: opens column div\n- Line 136: opens ResponsiveCard\n- Line 137: opens as-section-head\n- ... head content ...\n- Line ~150: closes as-section-head \n- Line ~151: opens as-section-body\n- ... body content (timeline entries) ...\n- Line ~185: closes as-section-body\n- Line 186: closes ResponsiveCard\n- Line 187: closes column div\n\nSo both sections look structurally correct.\n\nLet me try a different approach. Maybe the issue is that there's actually content after the closing PageShell tag or before the return statement that's causing the adjacent elements error.\n\nActually, let me look at the very beginning of the return statement and the very end.\n\nFrom line 130:\n```\n130\t  return (\n131\t    <PageShell title=\"Settings\" subtitle=\"Manage company timeline and team members\" error={error} onRetry={() =