 same location twice. Let me look at the exact line 240 in the file we have:\\n\\nWe can see that line 240 is the closing tag of the ResponsiveCard for the team member section.\\n\\nBut note that the timeline section also has a ResponsiveCard. Let me check the timeline section:\\n\\n135:          <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">\\n136:            <ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">\\n...\\n185:            </ResponsiveCard>\\n186:          </div>\\n\\nSo the timeline section\\'s ResponsiveCard is closed at line 185.\\n\\nNow, note that between the timeline section and the team section, we have:\\n\\n186:          </div>   <!-- timeline column -->\\n189:          <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">   <!-- team column -->\\n\\nWait, there is a missing closing tag for the timeline column? Actually, the timeline column is opened at line 135 and closed at line 186. Then the team column is opened at line 189 and closed at line 241.\\n\\nBut look at line 186 and 189: there is no content between them? Actually, there is a newline and some spaces, but no JSX. However, note that the timeline column div is closed at line 186, and then the team column div is opened at line 189. These are two adjacent div elements at the same level (inside the as-section-row). And that is the problem!\\n\\nWe have:\\n\\n        <div className=\"as-section-row row g-4\">\\n          {/* Timeline column */}\\n          <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">   <!-- opened at line 135 -->\\n            ... timeline card ...\\n          </div>   <!-- closed at line 186 -->\\n          {/* Team column -->  \\n          <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">   <!-- opened at line 189 -->\\n            ... team card ...\\n          </div>   <!-- closed at line 241 -->\\n        </div>\\n\\nBut wait, that is actually two div elements (the two columns) that are siblings. And that is allowed because they are both children of the as-section-row div. However, note that the error is about adjacent JSX elements. In JSX, you can have multiple siblings as long as they are all wrapped in a single parent. Here, the two columns are both children of the as-section-row div, so they are siblings and that is allowed.\\n\\nBut the error says adjacent JSX elements must be wrapped in an enclosing tag. This error would occur if we had, for example, two elements at the same level without a parent. In this case, the two columns have a parent (the as-section-row div). So why the error?\\n\\nWait, note that the as-section-row div is opened at line 133 and closed at line 242. So the structure is:\\n\\n<div className=\"as-section-row row g-4\">\\n  <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\"> ... </div>\\n  <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\"> ... </div>\\n</div>\\n\\nThat is valid.\\n\\nHowever, let\\'s look at the exact lines again. I suspect that there might be a missing closing tag for the timeline column\\'s div? But we see it at line 186.\\n\\nAlternatively, note that the timeline section\\'s ResponsiveCard is closed at line 185, and then we have:\\n\\n186:          </div>   <!-- This closes the timeline column div -->\\n187:          {/* \u2500\u2500 Team Members \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 -->   <-- This is a comment\\n188:          <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">   <!-- This opens the team column div -->\\n\\nSo between the timeline column div closing and the team column div opening, we have a comment. In JSX, comments are allowed and do not break the sibling relationship. So we have:\\n\\n  </div>   <!-- timeline column -->\\n  {/* comment */}\\n  <div className=\"col-12 col-sm-6\">   <!-- team column -->\\n\\nThis is still two adjacent div elements (with a comment in between) at the same level (inside the as-section-row). And that is allowed because they are both children of the as-section-row.\\n\\nBut wait, the error is saying that we have adjacent JSX elements that are not wrapped. However, note that the as-section-row div is the parent, so the two columns are not at the top level of the return statement. They are inside the as-section-row div.\\n\\nUnless... the as-section-row div is not properly closed? Let\\'s check:\\n\\nWe see the as-section-row div opened at line 133: <div className=\"as-section-row row g-4\">\\nAnd then we see it closed at line 242: </div>\\n\\nBut note that between the opening and closing, we have the two columns and the comment. So the as-section-row div is the parent of the two columns.\\n\\nHowever, the error is occurring at line 240, which is inside the team column. Let me look at the team column\\'s content again.\\n\\nWe see that the team column div is opened at line 189, and then we have:\\n\\n190:            <ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">\\n...\\n240:              </ResponsiveCard>\\n241:          </div>   <!-- This closes the team column div -->\\n\\nSo the team column div is closed at line 241, and then we have:\\n\\n242:        </div>   <!-- This closes the as-section-row div -->\\n243:      </div>   <!-- This closes the as-page div -->\\n244:    </PageShell>   <!-- This closes the PageShell -->\\n\\nSo the structure seems correct.\\n\\nBut wait, the error is at line 240:14, which is the closing tag of the ResponsiveCard inside the team column. The error says \"Adjacent JSX elements must be wrapped in an enclosing tag\". This error is being triggered by the ResponsiveCard\\'s closing tag? That doesn\\'t make sense.\\n\\nPerhaps the issue is that inside the team column\\'s ResponsiveCard, we have adjacent JSX elements? Let\\'s look at the content of the team column\\'s ResponsiveCard.\\n\\nWe have:\\n\\n190:            <ResponsiveCard className=\"as-section-card\">\\n191:              <div className=\"as-section-head\">\\n... \\n203:              </div>\\n204:              <div className=\"as-section-body\">\\n205:                <ResponsiveAlert ... />\\n206:                {loading.team ? (...) : team.length === 0 ? (...) : (\\n207:                  <div className=\"as-entries\">\\n208:                    {team.map((m, idx) => (\\n209:                      <div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>\\n210:                        <div className=\"as-entry-head\">\\n... \\n230:                        {m.bio && (\\n231:                          <div className=\"as-entry-body\">\\n... \\n236:                        )}\\n237:                      </div>\\n238:                    ))}\\n239:                  </div>\\n240:                )}\\n241:              </div>\\n242:            </ResponsiveCard>\\n\\nWait, note that at line 206, we have:\\n\\n                {loading.team ? (\\n                  <div className=\"as-loading\">...</div>\\n                ) : team.length === 0 ? (\\n                  <div className=\"as-empty\">...</div>\\n                ) : (\\n                  <div className=\"as-entries\">\\n                    {team.map(...)}\\n                  </div>\\n                )}\\n\\nThis is a ternary expression that returns JSX. However, note that the ternary expression is not wrapped in a div or fragment. But that is allowed because it is a single expression that returns one of three possible JSX elements.\\n\\nBut wait, the error is at line 240, which is the closing tag of the ResponsiveCard. Let me look at the lines around 240:\\n\\n239:                  </div>   <!-- This closes the as-entries div -->\\n240:                )}        <!-- This closes the ternary expression and the as-section-body div? -->\\n\\nActually, note that the as-section-body div is opened at line 204 and then we have the ternary expression. The ternary expression returns either the loading div, the empty div, or the as-entries div. Then we close the as-section-body div at line 241? Let\\'s see:\\n\\n204:              <div className=\"as-section-body\">\\n205:                <ResponsiveAlert ... />\\n206:                {loading.team ? (...) : team.length === 0 ? (...) : (...) }\\n207:              </div>   <!-- This is line 241? -->\\n\\nWait, we have to count the lines. Let me try to reconstruct the team member section from the file we have.\\n\\nAlternatively, note that the error might be because we have two adjacent elements in the as-section-body: the ResponsiveAlert and the ternary expression. But that is not allowed because they are two siblings without a wrapper.\\n\\nLook at lines 204-207:\\n\\n              <div className=\"as-section-body\">\\n                <ResponsiveAlert show={!!alert.team?.msg} onHide={() => clearAlert(\\'team\\')} message={alert.team?.msg} type={alert.team?.type} />\\n                {loading.team ? (...) : team.length === 0 ? (...) : (...) }\\n              </div>\\n\\nHere, inside the as-section-body div, we have two elements:\\n1. <ResponsiveAlert ... />\\n2. {loading.team ? (...) : team.length === 0 ? (...) : (...) }\\n\\nThese are two adjacent JSX elements (a component and a ternary expression that returns JSX) without being wrapped in a single parent. That is the cause of the error!\\n\\nWe must wrap the contents of the as-section-body div in a single parent, or use a fragment, or put them in a div.\\n\\nSimilarly, the same issue might exist in the timeline section.\\n\\nLet\\'s fix the timeline section first and then the team section.\\n\\nFor the timeline section, the as-section-body is:\\n\\n              <div className=\"as-section-body\">\\n                <ResponsiveAlert show={!!alert.timeline?.msg} onHide={() => clearAlert(\\'timeline\\')} message={alert.timeline?.msg} type={alert.timeline?.type} />\\n                {loading.timeline ? (...) : timeline.length === 0 ? (...) : (...) }\\n              </div>\\n\\nWe have the same issue: two adjacent elements (the alert and the ternary) inside the as-section-body div.\\n\\nWe can fix both by wrapping the two elements in a div, or by using a fragment. Since we are already in a div, we can just wrap them in another div, but that might add unnecessary nesting. Alternatively, we can use a fragment <>...</>.\\n\\nHowever, note that the as-section-body div is already there. We can change it to:\\n\\n              <div className=\"as-section-body\">\\n                <>\\n                  <ResponsiveAlert show={!!alert.timeline?.msg} onHide={() => clearAlert(\\'timeline\\')} message={alert.timeline?.msg} type={alert.timeline?.type} />\\n                  {loading.timeline ? (...) : timeline.length === 0 ? (...) : (...) }\\n                </>\\n              </div>\\n\\nBut note: the alert and the ternary are two elements, so we need to wrap them.\\n\\nAlternatively, we can put them in a div, but that would add an extra div. Since we are already in a div (the as-section-body), we can just remove the as-section-body div and put the two elements directly? But then we lose the class and the structure.\\n\\nAlternatively, we can keep the as-section-body div and put the two elements inside a fragment.\\n\\nLet\\'s do that for both sections.\\n\\nBut note: the error is occurring in the AdminSettings.jsx file, and we are currently looking at the team section. However, the timeline section might have the same issue and might cause an error after we fix the team section.\\n\\nLet\\'s fix the team section first.\\n\\nWe\\'ll change the as-section-body of the team member card from:\\n\\n              <div className=\"as-section-body\">\\n                <ResponsiveAlert show={!!alert.team?.msg} onHide={() => clearAlert(\\'team\\')} message={alert.team?.msg} type={alert.team?.type} />\\n                {loading.team ? (\\n                  <div className=\"as-loading\"><div className=\"as-spinner\" />Loading team members\u2026</div>\\n                ) : team.length === 0 ? (\\n                  <div className=\"as-empty\">\\n                    <div className=\"as-empty-icon\"><FaUsers /></div>\\n                    <div className=\"as-empty-text\">No team members yet</div>\\n                    <div className=\"as-empty-sub\">Add members to display on your public page</div>\\n                  </div>\\n                ) : (\\n                  <div className=\"as-entries\">\\n                    {team.map((m, idx) => (\\n                      <div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>\\n                        ... \\n                      </div>\\n                    ))}\\n                  </div>\\n                )}\\n              </div>\\n\\nto:\\n\\n              <div className=\"as-section-body\">\\n                <>\\n                  <ResponsiveAlert show={!!alert.team?.msg} onHide={() => clearAlert(\\'team\\')} message={alert.team?.msg} type={alert.team?.type} />\\n                  {loading.team ? (\\n                    <div className=\"as-loading\"><div className=\"as-spinner\" />Loading team members\u2026</div>\\n                  ) : team.length === 0 ? (\\n                    <div className=\"as-empty\">\\n                      <div className=\"as-empty-icon\"><FaUsers /></div>\\n                      <div className=\"as-empty-text\">No team members yet</div>\\n                      <div className=\"as-empty-sub\">Add members to display on your public page</div>\\n                    </div>\\n                  ) : (\\n                    <div className=\"as-entries\">\\n                      {team.map((m, idx) => (\\n                        <div className=\"as-entry-card\" key={m.id || idx}>\\n                          ... \\n                        </div>\\n                      ))}\\n                    </div>\\n                  )}\\n                </>\\n              </div>\\n\\nSimilarly for the timeline section.\\n\\nLet\\'s make these changes.\\n\\nFirst, let\\'s fix the team section (since the error is pointing to that area, but note the error is at line 240 which is the closing of the ResponsiveCard, but the actual problem is the two adjacent elements in the as-section-body).\\n\\nWe\\'ll edit the file accordingly.\\n\\nHowever, note that the error message for the build said the error was at line 240:14, which is the closing tag of the ResponsiveCard. But after we fix the two adjacent elements, the error should go away.\\n\\nLet\\'s do the change for the team section first.\\n\\nWe\\'ll read the current team section and then make the change.\\n\\nBut note: we are in the middle of the file. We\\'ll do an Edit from line 204 to line 241 (the as-section-body div and its contents) and replace it with the fixed version.\\n\\nAlternatively, we can do two separate edits: one for the timeline section and one for the team section.\\n\\nLet\\'s do the timeline section first, then the team section.\\n\\nFirst, timeline section:\\n\\nWe need to change the as-section-body of the timeline card (lines 150-183 in the current file?).\\n\\nLet me look at the timeline section in the current file:\\n\\n150:              <div className=\"as-section-body\">\\n151:                <ResponsiveAlert show={!!alert.timeline?.msg} onHide={() => clearAlert(\\'timeline\\')} message={alert.timeline?.msg} type={alert.timeline?.type} />\\n152:                {loading.timeline ? (\\n153:                  <div className=\"as-loading\"><div className=\"as-spinner\" />Loading timeline\u2026</div>\\n154:                ) : timeline.length === 0 ? (\\n155:                  <div className=\"as-empty\">\\n156:                    <div className=\"as-empty-icon\"><FaHistory /></div>\\n157:                    <div className=\"as-empty-text\">No timeline entries yet</div>\\n                    <div className=\"as-empty-sub\">Add your first milestone to showcase your journey</div>\\n                  </div>\\n                ) : (\\n                  <div className=\"as-entries\">\\n                    {timeline.map((item, idx) => (\\n             